The rx-ready and tx-ready mailbox handlers compute the transfer size by
packing three SDIO register bytes into a 24-bit value:

    size = (sdio_readb(func, 0x5c, NULL) << 16)
         | (sdio_readb(func, 0x7c, NULL) << 8)
         |  sdio_readb(func, 0x80, NULL);          // rx; tx is 0x60/0x84/0x88

That's wrong. Decompiled the real vendor driver (artosyn_sdio.ko, pulled
from a stock Ascent firmware dump's /usrdata/fpv/boot_ar8030/ -- the
actual driver this chip's RTOS firmware was written against) via Ghidra:
its equivalent irq handler reads a *single* byte from 0x5c (rx) / 0x60
(tx) and left-shifts by 9 (i.e. multiplies by 512, matching
SDIO_DEVICE_BLOCK_SIZE below) to get the byte count. 0x7c/0x80/0x84/0x88
aren't part of the real protocol at all -- this build was reading
whatever those addresses actually are and treating the result as a
transfer size.

This exact buggy decode is duplicated verbatim in two places:
artosyn_sdio_reg_check() (a rarely-exercised workqueue fallback,
triggered when artosyn_sdio_write()'s wait times out and suspects a
lost interrupt) and artosyn_sdio_irqhandler() (the function actually
registered via sdio_claim_irq() -- the one that runs on every real
SDIO interrupt). Both need the fix; having only the first one fixed
was indistinguishable from having neither fixed, since the second copy
is the one doing the real work.

Confirmed against a live UART capture off the AR8030's own debug pad,
comparing stock vs. this board's OpenIPC boot side by side: both track
identically through chip boot and RF calibration, but shortly after
"SDIO host interrupt enabled" the OpenIPC boot's *chip-side* firmware
starts logging "SDIO interrupt when tx not pending" / "SDIO read/write
over but no cb" -- protocol violations the chip's own firmware is
detecting from the host's behavior -- and a few keepalives later the
chip's hal_sdio_th task hard-faults (Load Instruction Access Error) and
the link is permanently dead. Stock's capture, same board, never once
logs any of that. This lines up exactly with the driver occasionally
handing the chip a garbage size born from reading unrelated registers,
until one such value corrupts something badly enough to crash the
chip's SDIO task outright.

diff --git a/driver/linux/bus/sdio.c b/driver/linux/bus/sdio.c
--- a/driver/linux/bus/sdio.c
+++ b/driver/linux/bus/sdio.c
@@ -1154,7 +1154,27 @@
             /* rx ready */
             if (mb_ena & (1<<2)) {
 #if 1
-                size = (sdio_readb(func, 0x5c, NULL) << 16) | (sdio_readb(func, 0x7c, NULL) << 8) | sdio_readb(func, 0x80, NULL);
+                /*
+                 * The size register at 0x5c is a *block count*, not a raw
+                 * byte count: the real vendor driver (artosyn_sdio.ko,
+                 * decompiled from the stock Ascent firmware) reads a
+                 * single byte here and left-shifts by 9 (i.e. * 512,
+                 * matching SDIO_DEVICE_BLOCK_SIZE below) to get the byte
+                 * size. The three-byte reconstruction this used to do --
+                 * 0x5c/0x7c/0x80 packed as a 24-bit value -- pulls 0x7c
+                 * and 0x80 out of thin air; they aren't part of the real
+                 * protocol at all, so read_valid_size/write_valid_size
+                 * ended up random noise from whatever those addresses
+                 * actually are. That's the real explanation for "works
+                 * for a few hundred packets, then the AR8030's own
+                 * hal_sdio_th firmware task hard-faults on a bad pointer"
+                 * -- confirmed via a live UART capture off the chip's
+                 * debug pad, comparing this board's stock vs. OpenIPC
+                 * boot: stock never logs "SDIO interrupt when tx not
+                 * pending" / "no cb"; this build did, right before the
+                 * crash, every single time.
+                 */
+                size = (unsigned int)sdio_readb(func, 0x5c, NULL) << 9;
                 if (read_int_handled || dev->read_offset != dev->read_valid_size) {
                     AR_DBG(DM_SDIO, DL_INF, "artosyn_sdio_reg_check %d-%d-%d-%d (%d %d) bytes for read lost\n", dev->read_valid_size, dev->read_offset, size, dev->read_size, read_int_handled, write_int_handled);
                 }
@@ -1173,7 +1193,8 @@

             /* tx ready */
             if (mb_ena & (1<<3)) {
-                size = (sdio_readb(func, 0x60, NULL) << 16) | (sdio_readb(func, 0x84, NULL) << 8) | sdio_readb(func, 0x88, NULL);
+                /* Same block-count-not-raw-bytes fix as the rx-ready case above. */
+                size = (unsigned int)sdio_readb(func, 0x60, NULL) << 9;
                 if (write_int_handled || dev->write_offset != dev->write_valid_size) {
                     AR_DBG(DM_SDIO, DL_INF, "%d-%d-%d-%d (%d %d) duplicate write interrupt\n", dev->write_valid_size, dev->write_offset, size, dev->write_size, read_int_handled, write_int_handled);
                 }
@@ -1274,7 +1295,13 @@
             if (mb_ena & (1<<2)) {
 #if 1
                 total_rx_isr++;
-                size = (sdio_readb(func, 0x5c, NULL) << 16) | (sdio_readb(func, 0x7c, NULL) << 8) | sdio_readb(func, 0x80, NULL);
+                /* See the identical fix + explanation in artosyn_sdio_reg_check() above --
+                 * this is the actual registered IRQ handler (sdio_claim_irq() below points
+                 * here, not at artosyn_sdio_reg_check()), so this copy is the one that
+                 * matters on every real interrupt. Missed fixing this copy the first time
+                 * around: two near-identical copies of the same buggy decode exist in this
+                 * file, and only the rarely-exercised workqueue fallback got fixed. */
+                size = (unsigned int)sdio_readb(func, 0x5c, NULL) << 9;
                 if (read_int_handled || dev->read_offset != dev->read_valid_size) {
                     AR_DBG(DM_SDIO, DL_WRN, "artosyn_sdio_irqhandler %d-%d-%d-%d (%d %d) bytes for read lost\n", dev->read_valid_size, dev->read_offset, size, dev->read_size, read_int_handled, write_int_handled);
                 }
@@ -1294,7 +1321,8 @@
             /* tx ready */
             if (mb_ena & (1<<3)) {
                 total_tx_isr++;
-                size = (sdio_readb(func, 0x60, NULL) << 16) | (sdio_readb(func, 0x84, NULL) << 8) | sdio_readb(func, 0x88, NULL);
+                /* Same block-count-not-raw-bytes fix as rx-ready above. */
+                size = (unsigned int)sdio_readb(func, 0x60, NULL) << 9;
                 if (write_int_handled || dev->write_offset != dev->write_valid_size) {
                     AR_DBG(DM_SDIO, DL_WRN, "%d-%d-%d-%d (%d %d) duplicate write interrupt\n", dev->write_valid_size, dev->write_offset, size, dev->write_size, read_int_handled, write_int_handled);
                 }
